.Dd December  8, 2004
.Dt KADMIND 8
.Os HEIMDAL
.Sh NAME
.Nm kadmind
.Nd "server for administrative access to Kerberos database"
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Bk -words
.Oo Fl c Ar file \*(Ba Xo
.Fl Fl config-file= Ns Ar file
.Xc
.Oc
.Oo Fl k Ar file \*(Ba Xo
.Fl Fl key-file= Ns Ar file
.Xc
.Oc
.Op Fl Fl keytab= Ns Ar keytab
.Oo Fl r Ar realm \*(Ba Xo
.Fl Fl realm= Ns Ar realm
.Xc
.Oc
.Op Fl d | Fl Fl debug
.Op Fl Fl detach
.Oo Fl p Ar port \*(Ba Xo
.Fl Fl ports= Ns Ar port
.Xc
.Oc
.Ek
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
listens for requests for changes to the Kerberos database and performs
these, subject to permissions.  When starting, if stdin is a socket it
assumes that it has been started by
.Xr inetd 8 ,
otherwise it behaves as a daemon, forking processes for each new
connection. The
.Fl Fl debug
option causes
.Nm
to accept exactly one connection, which is useful for debugging.
.Pp
The
.Xr kpasswdd 8
daemon is responsible for the Kerberos 5 password changing protocol
(used by
.Xr kpasswd 1 ) .
.Pp
This daemon should only be run on the master server, and not on any
slaves.
.Pp
Principals are always allowed to change their own password and list
their own principal.  Apart from that, doing any operation requires
permission explicitly added in the ACL file
.Pa /var/heimdal/kadmind.acl .
The format of this file is:
.Bd -ragged
.Va principal
.Va rights
.Op Va principal-pattern
.Ed
.Pp
Where rights is any (comma separated) combination of:
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
change-password or cpw
.It
list
.It
delete
.It
modify
.It
add
.It
get
.It
get-keys
.It
all (everything except get-keys)
.El
.Pp
And the optional
.Ar principal-pattern
restricts the rights to operations on principals that match the
glob-style pattern.
.Pp
Supported options:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl c Ar file , Fl Fl config-file= Ns Ar file
location of config file
.It Fl k Ar file , Fl Fl key-file= Ns Ar file
location of master key file
.It Fl Fl keytab= Ns Ar keytab
what keytab to use
.It Fl r Ar realm , Fl Fl realm= Ns Ar realm
realm to use
.It Fl d , Fl Fl debug
enable debugging
.It Fl Fl detach
detach from the terminal and run as a daemon; see
.Sx SERVICE MANAGER INTEGRATION .
.It Fl p Ar port , Fl Fl ports= Ns Ar port
ports to listen to. By default, if run as a daemon, it listens to port
749, but you can add any number of ports with this option. The port
string is a whitespace separated list of port specifications, with the
special string
.Dq +
representing the default port.
.El
.Sh SERVICE MANAGER INTEGRATION
.Nm
can be run by
.Xr systemd 1
or a compatible service manager in either of the two ways described
below, and either way can be combined with socket activation.
.Ss Type=forking
When started with
.Fl Fl detach ,
the process started by the service manager does not exit until the
daemon process that it starts is ready to serve requests, so the
service manager's notion of when
.Nm
is ready is accurate.
The daemon writes its process ID to
.Pa /var/run/kadmind.pid ,
or to
.Pa kadmind.pid
in the directory named by the
.Ev HEIM_PIDFILE_DIR
environment variable.
Use this with
.Li Type=forking
services, and set
.Li PIDFile=
accordingly (e.g.,
.Li PIDFile=/run/kadmind.pid
where
.Pa /var/run
is a symbolic link to
.Pa /run ) .
.Ss Type=notify
When the
.Ev NOTIFY_SOCKET
environment variable is set,
.Nm
sends
.Xr sd_notify 3
messages to the service manager:
.Li READY=1
once it is ready to serve requests,
.Li STATUS=
messages showing how many connections have been accepted, and
.Li STOPPING=1
when it begins shutting down.
Use this with
.Li Type=notify
services, with or without
.Fl Fl detach .
With
.Fl Fl detach ,
the process started by the service manager waits until the daemon
process is ready, then sends
.Li READY=1
and a
.Li MAINPID=
message naming the daemon process, and exits, so the service manager
tracks the daemon process from then on.
.Pp
.Li Type=forking
services that set
.Li NotifyAccess=
also receive these messages (e.g., to show the
.Li STATUS= ) ;
use
.Li NotifyAccess=exec
or
.Li NotifyAccess=all ,
as with
.Li NotifyAccess=main
systemd logs that it ignored the startup notification.
.Pp
.Nm
sends notifications only at startup, after accepting a connection, and
at shutdown, so they never cause additional wakeups.
In particular,
.Nm
does not send
.Li WATCHDOG=1
messages, so do not set
.Li WatchdogSec=
for it.
.Ss Socket activation
.Nm
supports systemd-style socket activation (see
.Xr sd_listen_fds 3 ) .
When started with sockets passed in this way it uses them instead of
creating its own, and the
.Fl Fl ports
option is ignored.
.Pp
If any of the passed sockets is named
.Dq kadmind
(e.g., with
.Li FileDescriptorName=kadmind
in the socket unit) then only those sockets are used; otherwise all
of the passed sockets are used.
Only listening IPv4 and IPv6 TCP sockets are used (i.e., the socket
unit must use
.Li Accept=no ,
the default).
Any other passed sockets (e.g., of another type or address family)
are closed with a warning, sockets not selected by name are closed
silently, and
.Nm
exits with an error if none of the passed sockets are usable.
.Pp
Socket activation works with both
.Li Type=forking
and
.Li Type=notify ;
with
.Fl Fl detach
the passed sockets are handed to the detached daemon process.
.Pp
Alternatively,
.Nm
can be run
.Xr inetd 8 Ns -style
by a socket unit with
.Li Accept=yes
and a service unit with
.Li StandardInput=socket ,
in which case a new
.Nm
process handles each connection.
.\".Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Sh FILES
.Pa /var/heimdal/kadmind.acl
.Sh EXAMPLES
This will cause
.Nm
to listen to port 4711 in addition to any
compiled in defaults:
.Pp
.D1 Nm Fl Fl ports Ns Li "=\*[q]+ 4711\*[q] &"
.Pp
This acl file will grant Joe all rights, and allow Mallory to view and
add host principals, as well as extract host principal keys (e.g., into
keytabs).
.Bd -literal -offset indent
joe/admin@EXAMPLE.COM      all
mallory/admin@EXAMPLE.COM  add,get-keys  host/*@EXAMPLE.COM
.Ed
.Sh CONFIGURATION FILE
kadmind uses the following configuration parameters from the
.Ar [kadmin]
section of
.Ar krb5.conf:
.Bl -tag -width Ds -offset indent
.It password_lifetime
.El
.Pp
kadmind uses the following configuration parameters from the per-realm entries
in the
.Ar [realms]
section of
.Ar krb5.conf:
.Bl -tag -width Ds -offset indent
.It supported_enctypes
.El
.Pp
kadmind uses the following configuration parameters from the
.Ar [password_quality]
section of
.Ar krb5.conf:
.Bl -tag -width Ds -offset indent
.It enforce_on_admin_set
.It min_length
.It min_classes
.It external_program
.It check_library
.It check_function
.It policy_libraries
.It policies
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr kpasswd 1 ,
.Xr kadmin 1 ,
.Xr kdc 8 ,
.Xr kpasswdd 8
